('values:lms_lesson:453','O:31:\"Drupal\\lms_lesson\\Entity\\Lesson\":30:{s:15:\"\0*\0entityTypeId\";s:10:\"lms_lesson\";s:15:\"\0*\0enforceIsNew\";N;s:12:\"\0*\0typedData\";N;s:17:\"\0*\0originalEntity\";N;s:16:\"\0*\0cacheContexts\";a:0:{}s:12:\"\0*\0cacheTags\";a:0:{}s:14:\"\0*\0cacheMaxAge\";i:-1;s:14:\"\0*\0_serviceIds\";a:0:{}s:18:\"\0*\0_entityStorages\";a:0:{}s:9:\"\0*\0values\";a:23:{s:2:\"id\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:3:\"453\";}s:4:\"uuid\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:36:\"5fb7dafa-ef38-4097-9731-69607da1180b\";}s:8:\"langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:2:\"en\";}s:4:\"name\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:38:\"Strategies for Handling Missing Values\";}s:13:\"field_content\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:2:{s:5:\"value\";s:1702:\"<h1>Introduction</h1><p>Handling missing values in datasets is a crucial step in data preparation, especially for AI applications. Missing data can significantly impact the performance of AI models, leading to biased or inaccurate results.</p><h2>Understanding Missing Values</h2><p>Missing values occur when no data value is stored for a variable in an observation. They can arise from various reasons, such as data entry errors, non-responses in surveys, or data corruption.</p><h2>Types of Missing Data</h2><ul><li><strong>Missing Completely at Random (MCAR):</strong> The likelihood of data being missing is unrelated to the data itself.</li><li><strong>Missing at Random (MAR):</strong> The missingness is related to some observed data but not the missing data itself.</li><li><strong>Missing Not at Random (MNAR):</strong> The missingness is related to the unobserved data.</li></ul><h2>Strategies for Handling Missing Values</h2><ol><li><strong>Deletion:</strong> Remove observations with missing values. Suitable for datasets with a small proportion of missing data.</li><li><strong>Imputation:</strong> Replace missing values with estimates. Common methods include mean, median, mode substitution, or more advanced techniques like K-nearest neighbors (KNN) imputation.</li><li><strong>Using Algorithms:</strong> Some machine learning algorithms can handle missing values natively, such as decision trees and random forests.</li></ol><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>Choosing the right strategy for handling missing values depends on the nature of the data and the specific requirements of the AI project. Careful consideration and testing are essential to ensure data integrity and model performance.</p>\";s:6:\"format\";s:9:\"full_html\";}}s:10:\"field_type\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:4:\"text\";}s:15:\"field_video_url\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:89:\"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=handling+missing+data+strategies+ai+datasets\";}s:16:\"field_video_file\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:21:\"field_zoom_meeting_id\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:19:\"field_zoom_schedule\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:19:\"field_scorm_package\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:14:\"field_duration\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:18:\"field_free_preview\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:15:\"field_drip_days\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:6:\"weight\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:6:\"status\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}s:7:\"created\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:10:\"1774442931\";}s:7:\"changed\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:10:\"1774519648\";}s:16:\"default_langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}s:10:\"field_quiz\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:21:\"field_pptx_attachment\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:14:\"field_comments\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:1:{i:0;a:6:{s:6:\"status\";s:1:\"2\";s:3:\"cid\";s:1:\"0\";s:22:\"last_comment_timestamp\";s:10:\"1774442931\";s:17:\"last_comment_name\";N;s:16:\"last_comment_uid\";s:1:\"0\";s:13:\"comment_count\";s:1:\"0\";}}}s:15:\"field_h5p_embed\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}}s:9:\"\0*\0fields\";a:0:{}s:19: