('values:lms_lesson:454','O:31:\"Drupal\\lms_lesson\\Entity\\Lesson\":30:{s:15:\"\0*\0entityTypeId\";s:10:\"lms_lesson\";s:15:\"\0*\0enforceIsNew\";N;s:12:\"\0*\0typedData\";N;s:17:\"\0*\0originalEntity\";N;s:16:\"\0*\0cacheContexts\";a:0:{}s:12:\"\0*\0cacheTags\";a:0:{}s:14:\"\0*\0cacheMaxAge\";i:-1;s:14:\"\0*\0_serviceIds\";a:0:{}s:18:\"\0*\0_entityStorages\";a:0:{}s:9:\"\0*\0values\";a:23:{s:2:\"id\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:3:\"454\";}s:4:\"uuid\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:36:\"b1bd2eab-8b10-467c-803a-78f2ae200cba\";}s:8:\"langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:2:\"en\";}s:4:\"name\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:39:\"Outlier Detection and Treatment Methods\";}s:13:\"field_content\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:2:{s:5:\"value\";s:1404:\"<h2>Understanding Outliers</h2><p>Outliers are data points that significantly differ from other observations. In data analysis, identifying and handling outliers is crucial to ensure accurate results.</p><h3>Types of Outliers</h3><p>Outliers can be classified into three categories: univariate, multivariate, and time-series. Each type requires different detection methods.</p><h3>Detection Methods</h3><ul><li><strong>Z-Score:</strong> A statistical measurement that describes a value\'s relationship to the mean of a group of values.</li><li><strong>IQR Method:</strong> Uses the interquartile range to identify data points that fall below the lower quartile or above the upper quartile.</li><li><strong>DBSCAN:</strong> A clustering-based method for identifying outliers as noise points in data.</li></ul><h3>Treatment Methods</h3><p>Handling outliers involves either removal or transformation. Common techniques include:</p><ul><li><strong>Trimming:</strong> Removing outliers from the dataset.</li><li><strong>Capping:</strong> Limiting extreme values to a specified range.</li><li><strong>Transformation:</strong> Applying mathematical functions to reduce the impact of outliers.</li></ul><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Choosing the right method for outlier detection and treatment is critical for the integrity of your data analysis. Always consider the context of your data before deciding on a method.</p>\";s:6:\"format\";s:9:\"full_html\";}}s:10:\"field_type\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:4:\"text\";}s:15:\"field_video_url\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:116:\"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=outlier+detection+methods%2C+z-score+outliers%2C+iqr+outliers+treatment\";}s:16:\"field_video_file\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:21:\"field_zoom_meeting_id\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:19:\"field_zoom_schedule\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:19:\"field_scorm_package\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:14:\"field_duration\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:18:\"field_free_preview\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:15:\"field_drip_days\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:6:\"weight\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:6:\"status\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}s:7:\"created\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:10:\"1774442931\";}s:7:\"changed\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:10:\"1774519648\";}s:16:\"default_langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}s:10:\"field_quiz\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:21:\"field_pptx_attachment\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:14:\"field_comments\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:1:{i:0;a:6:{s:6:\"status\";s:1:\"2\";s:3:\"cid\";s:1:\"0\";s:22:\"last_comment_timestamp\";s:10:\"1774442931\";s:17:\"last_comment_name\";N;s:16:\"last_comment_uid\";s:1:\"0\";s:13:\"comment_count\";s:1:\"0\";}}}s:15:\"field_h5p_embed\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}}s:9:\"\0*\0fields\";a:0:{}s:19:\"\0*\0fieldDefinitions\";N;s:12:\"\0*\0languages\";N;s:14:\"\0*\0langcodeKey\";s:8:\"langcode\";s:21:\"\0*\0defaultLangcodeKey\";s:16:\"default_langcode\";s:17:\"\0*\0activeLangcode\";s:9:\"x-default\";s:28:\"\0*\0enforceDefaultTranslation\";N;s:18:\"\0*\0defaultLangcode\";s:2:\"en\";s:15:\"\0*\0translations\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:1:{s:6:\"status\";i:1;}}s:24:\"\0*\0translationInitialize\";b:0;s:14:\"\0*\0newRevision\";b:0;s:20:\"\0*\0isDefaultRevision\";b:1;s:13:\"\0*\0entityKeys\";a:4:{s:6:\"bundle\";s:10:\"lms_lesson\";s:2:\"id\";s:3:\"454\";s:4:\"uuid\";s:36:\"b1bd2eab-8b10-467c-803a-78f2ae200cba\";s:9:\"published\";s:1:\"1\";}s:25:\"\0*\0translatableEntityKeys\";a:3:{s:5:\"label\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:39:\"Outlier Detection and Treatment Methods\";}s:8:\"langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:2:\"en\";}s:16:\"default_langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}}s:12:\"\0*\0validated\";b:0;s:21:\"\0*\0validationRequired\";b:0;s:19:\"\0*\0loadedRevisionId\";N;s:33:\"\0*\0revisionTranslationAffectedKey\";s:29:\"revision_translation_affected\";s:37:\"\0*\0enforceRevisionTranslationAffected\";a:0:{}s:12:\"\0*\0isSyncing\";b:0;}',-1,1774587141.928,1,'entity_field_info','0'),
('values:lms_lesson:455','O:31:\"Drupal\\lms_lesson\\Entity\\Lesson\":30:{s:15:\"\0*\0entityTypeId\";s:10:\"lms_lesson\";s:15:\"\0*\0enforceIsNew\";N;s:12:\"\0*\0typedData\";N;s:17:\"\0*\0originalEntity\";N;s:16:\"\0*\0cacheContexts\";a:0:{}s:12:\"\0*\0cacheTags\";a:0:{}s:14:\"\0*\0cacheMaxAge\";i:-1;s:14:\"\0*\0_serviceIds\";a:0:{}s:18:\"\0*\0_entityStorages\";a:0:{}s:9:\"\0*\0values\";a:23:{s:2:\"id\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:3:\"455\";}s:4:\"uuid\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:36:\"09eabb08-b5d5-4d2c-8244-54998d7f8dfb\";}s:8:\"langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:2:\"en\";}s:4:\"name\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:30:\"Data Transformation Techniques\";}s:13:\"field_content\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:2:{s:5:\"value\";s:1232:\"<h1>Data Transformation Techniques</h1><p>Data transformation is a key step in the data preprocessing phase of any AI project. It involves converting raw data into a format that is more suitable for analysis and modeling.</p><p>One common technique is scaling, which adjusts the range of data features. This is critical when dealing with algorithms sensitive to the scale of input data.</p><h2>Types of Data Transformation</h2><ul><li><strong>Normalization:</strong> This technique scales all numeric variables in the range of 0 to 1, ensuring that no feature dominates the others due to its scale.</li><li><strong>Standardization:</strong> This involves centering the data around zero with a standard deviation of one, useful for Gaussian distributions.</li><li><strong>Logarithmic Transformation:</strong> Applied to skewed data to reduce skewness, making it more normally distributed.</li></ul><p>Feature engineering is another aspect, where new features are created from existing ones to help improve model performance.</p><h3>Importance in AI</h3><p>Transforming data correctly can significantly impact the accuracy and efficiency of AI models. It ensures that data is in a form that allows algorithms to learn effectively.</p>\";s:6:\"format\";s:9:\"full_html\";}}s:10:\"field_type\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:4:\"text\";}s:15:\"field_video_url\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:151:\"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Data+Transformation+Techniques+for+AI%2C+Data+Scaling+and+Normalization%2C+Logarithmic+Data+Transformation\";}s:16:\"field_video_file\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:21:\"field_zoom_meeting_id\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:19:\"field_zoom_schedule\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:19:\"field_scorm_package\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:14:\"field_duration\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:18:\"field_free_preview\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:15:\"field_drip_days\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:6:\"weight\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:6:\"status\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}s:7:\"created\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:10:\"1774442931\";}s:7:\"changed\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:10:\"1774519648\";}s:16:\"default_langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}s:10:\"field_quiz\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:21:\"field_pptx_attachment\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:14:\"field_comments\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:1:{i:0;a:6:{s:6:\"status\";s:1:\"2\";s:3:\"cid\";s:1:\"0\";s:22:\"last_comment_timestamp\";s:10:\"1774442931\";s:17:\"last_comment_name\";N;s:16:\"last_comment_uid\";s:1:\"0\";s:13:\"comment_count\";s:1:\"0\";}}}s:15:\"field_h5p_embed\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}}s:9:\"\0*\0fields\";a:0:{}s:19:\"\0*\0fieldDefinitions\";N;s:12:\"\0*\0languages\";N;s:14:\"\0*\0langcodeKey\";s:8:\"langcode\";s:21:\"\0*\0defaultLangcodeKey\";s:16:\"default_langcode\";s:17:\"\0*\0activeLangcode\";s:9:\"x-default\";s:28:\"\0*\0enforceDefaultTranslation\";N;s:18:\"\0*\0defaultLangcode\";s:2:\"en\";s:15:\"\0*\0translations\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:1:{s:6:\"status\";i:1;}}s:24:\"\0*\0translationInitialize\";b:0;s:14:\"\0*\0newRevision\";b:0;s:20:\"\0*\0isDefaultRevision\";b:1;s:13:\"\0*\0entityKeys\";a:4:{s:6:\"bundle\";s:10:\"lms_lesson\";s:2:\"id\";s:3:\"455\";s:4:\"uuid\";s:36:\"09eabb08-b5d5-4d2c-8244-54998d7f8dfb\";s:9:\"published\";s:1:\"1\";}s:25:\"\0*\0translatableEntityKeys\";a:3:{s:5:\"label\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:30:\"Data Transformation Techniques\";}s:8:\"langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:2:\"en\";}s:16:\"default_langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}}s:12:\"\0*\0validated\";b:0;s:21:\"\0*\0validationRequired\";b:0;s:19:\"\0*\0loadedRevisionId\";N;s:33:\"\0*\0revisionTranslationAffectedKey\";s:29:\"revision_translation_affected\";s:37:\"\0*\0enforceRevisionTranslationAffected\";a:0:{}s:12:\"\0*\0isSyncing\";b:0;}',-1,1774587141.928,1,'entity_field_info','0'),
('values:lms_lesson:456','O:31:\"Drupal\\lms_lesson\\Entity\\Lesson\":30:{s:15:\"\0*\0entityTypeId\";s:10:\"lms_lesson\";s:15:\"\0*\0enforceIsNew\";N;s:12:\"\0*\0typedData\";N;s:17:\"\0*\0originalEntity\";N;s:16:\"\0*\0cacheContexts\";a:0:{}s:12:\"\0*\0cacheTags\";a:0:{}s:14:\"\0*\0cacheMaxAge\";i:-1;s:14:\"\0*\0_serviceIds\";a:0:{}s:18:\"\0*\0_entityStorages\";a:0:{}s:9:\"\0*\0values\";a:23:{s:2:\"id\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:3:\"456\";}s:4:\"uuid\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:36:\"43c383c4-b745-44ef-915e-c1babe559a67\";}s:8:\"langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:2:\"en\";}s:4:\"name\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:38:\"Data Normalization and Standardization\";}s:13:\"field_content\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:2:{s:5:\"value\";s:1609:\"<h2>Understanding Data Normalization and Standardization</h2><p>Data normalization and standardization are essential techniques in data preprocessing, crucial for improving the performance and stability of machine learning models. These processes ensure that data is formatted consistently, allowing algorithms to interpret and analyze data effectively.</p><h3>What is Data Normalization?</h3><p>Normalization is the process of scaling individual samples to have unit norm. This step ensures that each input variable contributes equally to the distance computations. It typically transforms data to a range of [0, 1].</p><ul><li>Used when you know the distribution of your data does not follow a Gaussian distribution.</li><li>Suitable for algorithms that do not assume any distribution of the data.</li></ul><h3>What is Data Standardization?</h3><p>Standardization involves rescaling the features so that they have the properties of a standard normal distribution with a mean of zero and a standard deviation of one. This process is crucial when the data follows a Gaussian distribution.</p><ul><li>Helps in centering the data around the mean.</li><li>Suitable for algorithms that assume Gaussian distribution of the data.</li></ul><h3>Benefits of Normalization and Standardization</h3><ol><li>Improves the convergence speed of gradient descent algorithms.</li><li>Enhances the performance of many machine learning algorithms.</li><li>Helps in reducing the effects of outliers in data.</li></ol><p>Understanding these techniques and when to apply them is vital for data scientists working on AI projects.</p>\";s:6:\"format\";s:9:\"full_html\";}}s:10:\"field_type\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:4:\"text\";}s:15:\"field_video_url\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:96:\"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=data+normalization+standardization+AI+preprocessing\";}s:16:\"field_video_file\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:21:\"field_zoom_meeting_id\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:19:\"field_zoom_schedule\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:19:\"field_scorm_package\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:14:\"field_duration\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:18:\"field_free_preview\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:15:\"field_drip_days\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:6:\"weight\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"0\";}s:6:\"status\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}s:7:\"created\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:10:\"1774442931\";}s:7:\"changed\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:10:\"1774519648\";}s:16:\"default_langcode\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";s:1:\"1\";}s:10:\"field_quiz\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}s:21:\"field_pptx_attachment\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:3:{s:9:\"target_id\";N;s:7:\"display\";N;s:11:\"description\";N;}}s:14:\"field_comments\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";a:1:{i:0;a:6:{s:6:\"status\";s:1:\"2\";s:3:\"cid\";s:1:\"0\";s:22:\"last_comment_timestamp\";s:10:\"1774442931\";s:17:\"last_comment_name\";N;s:16:\"last_comment_uid\";s:1:\"0\";s:13:\"comment_count\";s:1:\"0\";}}}s:15:\"field_h5p_embed\";a:1:{s:9:\"x-default\";N;}}s:9:\"\0*\0fields\";a:0:{}s:19:\"\0*\0fieldDefinitions\";N;s:12:\"\0*\0languages\";N;s:14:\"\0*\0langcodeKey\";s:8:\"